      "title": "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"
    },
    "markdown": "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{x-7}.$",
    "mathAcademyId": "q-67421",
    "preview": true,
    "requiresCalculator": null,
    "solution": "First, we note that $x=7$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{6}{1}=\\frac{x-7}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-6=x-7\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =x-7 \\\\ -6+7 & =x-7+7 \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=1.$",
    "status": "incorrect",
